Since you're on .NET 10, you already have the built-in **`TimeProvider`** — no custom wrapper or extra NuGet needed. Here's how to adopt it:

## 1. Inject `TimeProvider` into `ReportGenerator`

Update your class to accept `TimeProvider` via constructor injection:

```csharp
namespace NeedsWrappers.Services;

public class ReportGenerator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var timestamp = timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("yyyyMMdd_HHmmss");
        var outputDir = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("REPORT_OUTPUT_DIR") ?? "/tmp/reports";

        if (!Directory.Exists(outputDir))
        {
            Directory.CreateDirectory(outputDir);
        }

        var reportPath = Path.Combine(outputDir, $"{reportName}_{timestamp}.txt");
        var content = $"Report: {reportName}\nGenerated: {timeProvider.GetUtcNow():O}\nMachine: {Environment.MachineName}";

        File.WriteAllText(reportPath, content);
        Console.WriteLine($"Report written to {reportPath}");

        return reportPath;
    }
}
```

## 2. Register in DI (Program.cs / Startup)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
```

## 3. Write deterministic tests with `FakeTimeProvider`

Install the test helper package in your test project:

```
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

Then in your tests:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing;

var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 24, 12, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var generator = new ReportGenerator(fakeTime);
var path = generator.GenerateReport("Sales");

// Timestamp is now deterministic: "20260624_120000"
Assert.Contains("Sales_20260624_120000", path);

// You can also advance time:
fakeTime.Advance(TimeSpan.FromHours(1));
```

**Key points:**
- Replace `DateTime.UtcNow` → `timeProvider.GetUtcNow()` (returns `DateTimeOffset`)
- Production code uses `TimeProvider.System` (real clock)
- Tests use `FakeTimeProvider` (controllable, deterministic)

> **Note:** Your class also uses `File.*`, `Directory.*`, `Environment.*`, and `Console.*` statics — those will need separate abstractions if you want full testability. Let me know if you'd like wrappers for those too.
